(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-oxygen-delivery-quiz"]={"title":"Oxygen Delivery Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which interfaces deliver air-entrainment nebulizer oxygen over the face?","choices":["Bag-valve mask and resuscitator bag","Aerosol mask and open face mask","Nasal cannula and simple mask","Partial and non-rebreather masks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The aerosol mask and the open face mask are the interfaces that deliver air-entrainment nebulizer oxygen over the face. The T-piece and the trach collar are interfaces for tubes. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which hazard of oxygen therapy is a risk for anyone near the patient, not only the patient?","choices":["Mucosal irritation","Vasoconstriction","Fire","Hypercapnia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fire is the hazard that threatens anyone near the patient, not only the patient, because oxygen is highly flammable. The others (hypercapnia, mucosal irritation, vasoconstriction) injure only the person receiving the oxygen. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"At rest on room air, a saturation at or below what cutoff qualifies a patient for home oxygen with no comorbidity required?","choices":["84%","88%","92%","96%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A resting room air oxygen saturation at or below 88% qualifies a patient for home oxygen without any comorbidity, which places the patient in Group I. A value just above that, 89%, needs a chronic disease complication to qualify. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How soon is a patient recertified who qualifies only because of a moderately abnormal value plus a chronic disease complication?","choices":["The conditional group is rechecked at 3 months","The conditional group is rechecked yearly","The conditional group is exempt from rechecks","The conditional group is rechecked weekly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A borderline value plus pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or polycythemia places the patient in the conditional group (Group II), and that group is recertified at 3 months instead of 12. The chronic disease complication prompts the earlier recheck. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A non-rebreather mask at 10 to 15 L/min delivers what fraction of inspired oxygen?","choices":["60% to 100%","24% to 44%","35% to 50%","24% to 50%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A non-rebreather mask at 10 to 15 L/min delivers 60% to 100% fraction of inspired oxygen. It is a low flow device with a high fraction, and it is used in severely hypoxic patients who are still ventilating well. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why is oxygen withheld in myocardial infarction unless the saturation is low?","choices":["It causes carbon dioxide retention","It irritates the nasal mucosa","It is a fire hazard at the bedside","It causes vasoconstriction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is withheld in myocardial infarction unless the saturation is low because it causes vasoconstriction, which is unwelcome when the heart is short of blood flow. The caution lifts only when the saturation is below 91%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Why is the Venturi mask often used in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["It delivers up to 100% oxygen","It runs without oxygen flow","It lets exhaled gas refill the bag","Its fraction can be dialed in precisely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A Venturi mask allows precise delivery of oxygen because different ports change the fraction, so it can be dialed in to the patient's oxygen requirement. That precision is why it is often used in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which delivery device is classed as a high flow device?","choices":["Nasal cannula","Simple mask","Non-rebreather mask","Venturi mask"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Venturi mask is classed as a high flow device, and it allows precise delivery of oxygen, with different ports changing the fraction delivered. The flow is adjusted from 4 to 10 L/min to give 24% to 50%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What fraction of inspired oxygen does room air provide?","choices":["16%","21%","24%","28%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Room air provides a fraction of inspired oxygen of .21, or 21%. That is the baseline from which every oxygen device adds, as in the nasal cannula's 24% at 1 L/min and 28% at 2 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Across which fraction of inspired oxygen can a Venturi mask be set?","choices":["24% to 50%","21% to 30%","40% to 70%","60% to 100%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A Venturi mask allows the fraction of inspired oxygen to be set across 24% to 50% by changing the port, with the flow adjusted from 4 to 10 L/min. This precision is why it is often used in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"During which phase of a respiratory illness is nebulizer therapy listed as a rationale?","choices":["The recovery after discharge","The first mild cough","A severe exacerbation","The symptom-free period"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A severe exacerbation of a respiratory illness is a listed rationale for nebulizer therapy. The others include direct delivery of liquid medication and secretion clearance. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"On a Venturi mask, what changes the fraction of inspired oxygen delivered?","choices":["Inflating the reservoir bag","Tightening the head strap","Opening a one-way valve","Changing the port"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. On a Venturi mask, different ports change the fraction of inspired oxygen delivered, and the flow can be adjusted from 4 to 10 L/min to create 24% to 50%. The port is the control. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which two airways are listed as tracheal airways?","choices":["Oropharyngeal and nasopharyngeal","Bag-valve mask and resuscitator bag","Aerosol mask and open face mask","Endotracheal tube and tracheostomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The endotracheal tube and the tracheostomy are the two listed as tracheal airways. The oropharyngeal and nasopharyngeal airways are listed separately as artificial airways. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What must happen during exercise for a saturation of 88% or lower to qualify a patient for ambulatory oxygen?","choices":["It recovers on room air within a minute","It stays the same on oxygen and room air","It falls further once oxygen is added","It improves when oxygen is given"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. During exertion, a saturation of 88% or lower qualifies a patient for ambulatory oxygen only if it improves when oxygen is given. The improvement shows that the oxygen helps the patient. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the minimum distance to keep home oxygen from a heat source such as a candle or pilot light?","choices":["At least 2 feet","At least 5 feet","At least 10 feet","At least 50 feet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen should be kept at least 10 feet from heat sources such as pilot lights, electric appliances and candles. Oxygen is highly flammable, so a safe distance matters. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A nasal cannula set at 3 L/min delivers about what fraction of inspired oxygen?","choices":["24%","32%","36%","44%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A nasal cannula at 3 L/min delivers about 32% fraction of inspired oxygen, following the per-liter list: 24%, 28%, 32%, 36%, 40% and 44% for 1 through 6 L/min, in 4% steps. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which products should a patient on home oxygen avoid using on the face?","choices":["Unscented soaps and plain water","Cotton tissues and soft cloths","Petroleum-based lip balms and lotions","Water-based saline nasal sprays"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients on home oxygen should avoid petroleum-based products such as lip balms, lotions, oils and grease. Oxygen is highly flammable, which is why these products are on the avoid list. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which delivery system gives a fixed fraction of inspired oxygen that does not fluctuate with the patient's demand?","choices":["Air-entrainment system","Nasal cannula","Simple mask","Non-rebreather mask"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An air-entrainment system gives a fixed fraction of inspired oxygen that does not fluctuate with the patient's demand. The other devices listed vary with the patient's breathing or demand. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which tools are shown for exchanging or guiding an endotracheal tube?","choices":["A tube changer and a bougie","A Venturi port and adapter","A reservoir bag and valve","A nebulizer cup and tubing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A tube changer and a bougie are the tools shown for exchanging or guiding an endotracheal tube. They belong with the tracheal airways, the endotracheal tube and the tracheostomy. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What signage should a patient on home oxygen post in the home?","choices":["A sign naming the oxygen supplier","A sign with the recertification date","No Smoking and Oxygen in Use signs","A sign listing the oxygen flow rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A patient on home oxygen should post No Smoking signage and potentially Oxygen in Use signs within the home. These signs follow from oxygen being highly flammable, a fire hazard. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"On an arterial blood gas, an arterial oxygen pressure at or below what cutoff qualifies a patient for home oxygen with no comorbidity required?","choices":["50 mm Hg","55 mm Hg","59 mm Hg","65 mm Hg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An arterial oxygen pressure at or below 55 mm Hg qualifies for home oxygen without any comorbidity (Group I). Readings of 56 to 59 mm Hg qualify only when pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or polycythemia is documented. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How often is Group I home oxygen recertified?","choices":["Every 1 month","Every 3 months","Every 12 months","Every 24 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Group I home oxygen, where the oxygen value qualifies on its own without a chronic disease complication, is recertified every 12 months. The conditional group, Group II, is recertified earlier, at 3 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"If a nasal cannula is turned above 6 L/min, what happens to the fraction of inspired oxygen?","choices":["It stays at about 44%","It climbs past 60%","It falls back toward 21%","It reaches 100%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A nasal cannula can be turned above 6 L/min, but the fraction of inspired oxygen maxes out at 44%, so the extra flow adds nothing. The usable range stays 1 to 6 L/min, giving 24% to 44%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A resting arterial oxygen pressure of 56 to 59 mm Hg qualifies for home oxygen only with evidence of which finding?","choices":["Seasonal allergies","Controlled hypertension","A recent upper respiratory infection","Cor pulmonale"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An arterial oxygen pressure of 56 to 59 mm Hg is the conditional Group II range, and it qualifies only with pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale (right heart failure) or hematocrit above 56%. Cor pulmonale is the finding offered here. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which acute medical or surgical period is named as an indication for oxygen therapy?","choices":["The first trimester of pregnancy","The week after a routine vaccine","Recovery after a simple sprain","The perioperative and postoperative period"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The perioperative and postoperative period is the acute medical or surgical need named as an indication for oxygen therapy. The same list also names hypoxia, carbon monoxide poisoning, air hunger and cluster headache. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which respiratory condition is listed as an indication for oxygen therapy even though the problem is the gas the patient breathed, not the lungs?","choices":["Iron deficiency anemia","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Spontaneous pneumothorax","Seasonal allergic rhinitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The stem points to a problem with the gas the patient breathed, not the lungs, and carbon monoxide poisoning is the respiratory condition listed as an indication for oxygen therapy. The other listed indications are hypoxia, acute medical or surgical needs and the special indications. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which clinical assessment findings are used to judge the response to oxygen therapy?","choices":["Symptoms, work of breathing, color and perfusion","Appetite, sleep quality and weight","Hair growth, nail shape and skin turgor","Reflexes, pupil size and grip strength"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The clinical assessment of response to oxygen therapy covers symptom improvement, work of breathing, and color and perfusion. Objective data such as saturation and the blood gas follow. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What does the one-way valve on a non-rebreather mask do?","choices":["Stops the patient inhaling exhaled air","Mixes room air in at a fixed ratio","Adds humidity to the oxygen flow","Releases medication into the mask"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The one-way valve on a non-rebreather mask prevents the patient from inhaling exhaled air. The reservoir bag then supplies a high concentration of oxygen, 60% to 100% at 10 to 15 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Besides cluster headache, which symptom is a special indication for oxygen therapy?","choices":["Chest wall pain","Nasal congestion","Air hunger","Chronic cough"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Air hunger is the other special indication for oxygen therapy, listed together with cluster headache. It is a symptom indication, so the patient may receive oxygen for relief of the sensation of breathlessness. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Above what nasal cannula flow rate is humidity added?","choices":["1 L/min","4 L/min","6 L/min","10 L/min"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Humidity is used with nasal cannula flow rates greater than 4 L/min. Since the cannula runs only from 1 to 6 L/min, that means the 5 and 6 L/min settings need humidity added. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 9"}]};
